✅ How JavaScript Handles Function Calls with Varying Arguments
In JavaScript, functions are flexible:

If fewer arguments are passed than declared parameters, the missing ones become undefined.

If more arguments are passed, they’re simply ignored unless captured using the arguments object or rest parameter.

function flexibleFunction(a, b, ...rest) {
  console.log("a:", a);
  console.log("b:", b);
  console.log("rest:", rest);
}



And these calls:

flexibleFunction(1);
// a: 1, b: undefined, rest: []

flexibleFunction(1, 2);
// a: 1, b: 2, rest: []

flexibleFunction(1, 2, 3, 4, 5);
// a: 1, b: 2, rest: [3, 4, 5]


✅ What is the Rest Parameter?
The rest parameter (...rest) allows a function to accept an indefinite number of arguments as an array.

Syntax:
function myFunc(a, b, ...rest) {
  // rest is an array of extra arguments beyond a and b
}


✅ sumAll Function using Rest Parameter

function sumAll(...numbers) {
  return numbers.reduce((sum, current) => sum + current, 0);
}

// Usage:
console.log(sumAll(1, 2, 3));       // 6
console.log(sumAll(10, 20, 30, 40)); // 100
console.log(sumAll());              // 0



✅ Challenge: processArguments
function processArguments(primaryFunc, ...args) {
  return primaryFunc(...args);
}

// Example usage:
function multiply(x, y) {
  return x * y;
}

console.log(processArguments(multiply, 4, 5));      // 20
console.log(processArguments(Math.max, 1, 3, 7));   // 7
console.log(processArguments(sumAll, 10, 20, 30));  // 60
